","To calculate the dose of the drug, we first need to find the patient's body surface area (BSA). The BSA can be calculated using the formula:
\[
\text{BSA} = 0.15 \times \sqrt{\text{weight (kg)} \times \text{height (cm)}}
\]
First, convert the patient's weight from pounds to kilograms and height from feet and inches to centimeters.
Weight:
\[
65 \text{ lb} \times 0.453592 \text{ kg/lb} = 29.695 \text{ kg}
\]
Height:
\[
3 \text{ ft} \times 12 \text{ cm/ft} + 6 \text{ in} \times 2.54 \text{ cm/in} = 36 + 15.6 = 51.6 \text{ cm}
\]
Now, calculate the BSA:
\[
\text{BSA} = 0.15 \times \sqrt{29.695 \times 51.6} = 0.15 \times \sqrt{1551.11 \text{ m}^2} = 0.15 \times 39.42 \text{ m}^2 = 5.963 \text{ m}^2
\]
The dose of the drug is 17.5 mg/m²/day. To find the daily dose for the patient, multiply the BSA by the dose per m²:
\[
\text{Daily dose} = 17.5 \text{ mg/m}^2 \times 5.963 \text{ m}^2 = 104.1525 \text{ mg}
\]
