<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)) for the electrochemical reaction, we use the Nernst equation:
\[
\Delta G = -n F \Delta E
\]
where:
- \(n\) is the number of electrons transferred in the reaction.
- \(F\) is the Faraday constant (96,410 C/mol).
- \(\Delta E\) is the standard cell potential (\(\Delta E\)).

First, calculate the standard cell potential (\(\Delta E\)) using the standard standard reduction potentials:
\[
\Delta E = E_{oxidant} - E_{reductant}
\]
In this case, the cathode is the reduction reaction (\(Cu^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow Cu\)) and the anode is the oxidation reaction (\(Zn \rightarrow Zn^{2+} + 2e^-\)). Thus, the standard cell potential is:
\[
\Delta E = E_{Cu^2^+} - E_{Zn^{2+}} = 0.34 \, \text{V} - (-0.76 \, \text{V}) = 1.10 \, \text{V}
\]

Next, determine the number of electrons transferred (\(n\)). The balanced equation is:
\[
Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)
\]
Two electrons are transferred in this reaction (Zn loses 2 electrons and Cu\(2+\) gains 2 electrons). Thus, \(n = 2\).

Finally, calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)):
\[
\Delta G = -n F \Delta E = -2 \times 96,410 \, \text{C/mol} \times 1.10 \, \text{V} = -212,822 \, \text{J/mol}
\]
Converting to kJ/mol:
\[
\Delta G = -212,822 \, \text{J/mol} \times \frac{1000 \, \text{J}}{1 \, \text{kJ}} = -212.822 \, \text{kJ/mol}
