","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^2 \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the following reaction occurs:
\[ \text{H}_2\text{S} + \text{M}^2^+ \rightarrow \text{M}^2^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \]
The reaction is favored when the \( K_f \) for the formation of the sulfide is high. The \( K_f \) values for the formation of metal sulfides are given by:
\[ K_f = \frac{K_w}{K_s} \]
where \( K_w = 10^{-14} \) is the ion product of water and \( K_s \) is the solubility product of the metal sulfide.

The solubility products of the metal sulfides are:
\[
\begin{array{|l|r|}
\hline
\text{Metal sulfide} & K_s \\
\hline
\text{MnS} & 1.0 \times 10^{-14} \\
\text{NiS} & 1.0 \times 10^{16} \\
\text{CuS} & 1.0 \times 10^{13} \\
\text{HgS} & 1.0 \times 10^{16} \\
\hline
\end{array}
\]
The \( K_f \) values for the formation of the metal sulfides are:
\[
\begin{array{|l|r|}
\hline
\text{Metal sulfide} & K_f \\
\hline
\text{MnS} & 10^{14} \\
\text{NiS} & 10^{-34} \\
\text{CuS} & 10^{-11} \\
\text{HgS} & 10^{-34} \\
\hline
end{array}
\]
Since the \( K_f \) values for NiS and HgS are very low, these sulfides will precipitate. The \( K_f \) value for CuS is also low, so it will precipitate as well. The \( K_f \) value for MgS is high, so it will not precipitate.

The order of precipitation is:
1. \(\text{HgS}\) (highest \( K_f \))
2. \(\text{NiS}\) (high \( K_f \))
3. \(\text{CuS}\) (medium \( K_f \))
4. \(\text{MgS}\) (low \( K_f \))

