The concentration and duration of exposure are interdependent factors that influence the rate of photochemical reactions. The rate of the reaction is proportional to the product of the concentration of the photosensitizer and the duration of exposure, assuming the light intensity is constant. This means that the rate of the reaction can be increased by increasing either the concentration of the photosensitizer or the duration of exposure, or both.

