A three-step mechanism has been suggested for the formation of carbonyl chloride:
- Step I: \( \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{Cl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step II: \( \text{Cl} + \text{CO} \rightarrow \text{COCl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step III: \( \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \) (slow)

What is the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The rate-determining step is the slowest step in the mechanism, which is given as Step III:
\[ \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \]
The rate-determining step involves the reaction of COCl with Cl₂ to form COCl₂ and Cl.

The molecularity of a reaction is the number of molecules that react in a single step. In this case, the rate-determining step involves two molecules:
1. COCl (one molecule)
2. Cl₂ (one molecule)

Since two molecules react in the rate-determining step, the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III) is **bifunctional** (or bimolecular).

Thus, the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III) is \(\boxed{\text{bimolecular}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

