","If \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = 0 \), then \( \sum X^2 = 0 \).
If \( X \) is a discrete random variable, then \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = \sum x^2 p(x) = 0 \), where \( p(x) \) is the probability mass function.
If \( X \) is a continuous random variable, then \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = \int x^2 f(x) \, dx = 0 \), where \( f(x)) \) is the probability density function.

In either case, we have \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = 0 \).
Since \( X^2 \geq 0 \), the probability mass function \( p(x) \) must be zero for all \( x \neq 0 \).
For the discrete case, we have
\[ \sum x^2 p(x) = 0 \implies \sum x^2 p(x) = 0 \).
Since \( X^2 \geq 0 \), we have \( x^2 p(x) \geq 0 \).
Thus, the sum can be zero only if \( x^2 p(x) = 0 \) for all \( x \).
If \( x \neq 0 \), then \( x^2 \neq 0 \), so \( p(x) = 0 \).
For \( X = 0 \), we have \( 0^2 p(0) = 0 \), so \( p(0) = 0 \) is possible.
However, the sum must sum to 1, so \(\sum p(x) = 1 \).
\[ \sum p(x) = p(0) + \sum_{x \neq 0} p(x) = p(0) + 0 = 1 \).
Thus, \( p(0) = 1 \).

For the continuous case, we have
\[ \int x^2 f(x) \, dx = 0 \).
Since \( x^2 f(x) \geq 0 \), the integral can be zero only if \( x^2 f(x) = 0 \) for all \( x \).
If \( x \neq 0 \), then \( x^2 \neq 0 \), so \( f(x) = 0 \).
For \( x = 0 \), we have \( 0^2 f(0) = 0 \), so \( f(0) \) is possible.
However, the sum must sum to 1, so \(\sum f(x) = 1 \).
\[ \int f(x) \, dx = f(0) + \sum_{x \neq 0} f(x) = f(0) + 0 = 1 \).
Thus, \( f(0) = 1 \).

Thus, in both cases, we have \( \mathbb{P}(X = 0) = 1 \).

