","In stratified sampling, the sample is divided into groups (strata) based on the type of dairy product. The number of samples from each stratum is proportional to the number of units in the stratum.
The total number of brands is:
\[
30 + 10 + 35 + 25 = 100
\]
The proportions of each type of dairy product are:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(\frac{30}{100} = 0.3\)
        \item Yogurt: \(\frac{10}{100} = 0.1\)
        \item Infant formula: \(\frac{35}{100} = 0.35\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(\frac{25}{100} = 0.25\)
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
The total sample size is $n$. The number of samples from each type is proportional to the proportion of the type in the total number of brands.
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(\frac{30}{100} \times n = 0.3n\)
        \item Yogurt: \(\frac{10}{100} \times n = 0.1n\)
        \itemize
    \end{itemize
    \item Infant formula: \(\frac{35}{100} \times n = 0.35n\)
    \item Adult milk powder: \(\frac{25}{100} \times n = 0.25n\)
\end{itemize}
We are given that 7 brands of infant formula are sampled, so
\[
0.35n = 7
\]
Solving for \(n\):
\[
n = \frac{7}{0.35} = \frac{7}{35/100} = \frac{7 \times 100}{35} = \frac{700}{35} = 20
\]
Thus, the total sample size is \(n = 0.35 \times 20 = 7\).
