If \( X \) is a continuous random variable, then \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = \int x^2 f(x) \, dx = 0 \), where \( f(x)) \) is the probability density function.

In either case, we have \( \mathbb{E}(X^2) = 0 \).
Since \( X^2 \geq 0 \), the probability mass function \( p(x) \) must be zero for all \( x \neq 0 \).
