Thus, the sum can be zero only if \( x^2 p(x) = 0 \) for all \( x \).
If \( x \neq 0 \), then \( x^2 \neq 0 \), so \( p(x) = 0 \).
For \( X = 0 \), we have \( 0^2 p(0) = 0 \), so \( p(0) = 0 \) is possible.
However, the sum must sum to 1, so \(\sum p(x) = 1 \).
\[ \sum p(x) = p(0) + \sum_{x \neq 0} p(x) = p(0) + 0 = 1 \).
Thus, \( p(0) = 1 \).

For the continuous case, we have
\[ \int x^2 f(x) \, dx = 0 \).
Since \( x^2 f(x) \geq 0 \), the integral can be zero only if \( x^2 f(x) = 0 \) for all \( x \).
