An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), follow these steps:

1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}^{(0)} \)**:
   The closed-loop gain with negative feedback is given by the formula:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   \[
   A_{vf^{(0)}} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 109.09 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new open-loop gain \( A_v' \)**:
   The gain decreases by 6 dB due to aging. The gain is reduced to \( \frac{1}{2} \), since \( 10^{-6} = 1/2 \).
   \[
   A_v' = \frac{1}{2} \times 100 = 50 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \)**:
   Using the same feedback factor \( m_v = 0.1 \) and the new open-loop gain \( A_v' = 50 \):
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{50}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{50}{0.9} \approx 55.56 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain**:
   \[
   \Delta A_{vf} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf^{(0)}}{ A_{vf^{(0)}}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{55.56 - 109.09}{109.09} \right) \times 100
   \]
   \[
   \Delta A_{vf} = \left( \frac{-53.53}{109.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -48.5\%
   \]

