","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the force on the proton divided by its mass. The force on the proton is given by the electric field multiplied by the charge of the proton:
\[ F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}. \]
The acceleration is then given by
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2. \]

(b) The proton starts at rest, so its initial velocity is \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). The final velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the kinematic equation \(v = v_0 + a t\), we have
\[ 35,000 \, \text{m/s} = 0 \, \text{m/s} + (4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2) t. \]
Solving for \(t\), we get
\[ t = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.5 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s}. \]

Final Answer:
(a) \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\)
