<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton scattering formula:

\[
\lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} + \frac{2\lambda_{\text{scatt}}}{m_{\text{e}}} \cos \theta
\]

where:
\[
\lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \text{Wavelength of the scattered photon}
\]
\[
\lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \text{Wavelength of the incident photon}
\]
\[
m_{\text{e}} = \text{Mass of the electron} = 9.1 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg}
\]
\[
\theta = \text{Angle of scattering}
\]

The maximum percentage change in wavelength occurs when the scattering angle is \(\theta = 0^\circ\) (head-on scattering). In this case, \(\cos \theta = 1\). The change in wavelength is:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} - \lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \frac{2\lambda_{\text{scatt}}}{m_{\text{e}}}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10}}{9.1 \times 10^{-3}} = 4.17 \times 10^{-7} \text{ Å}
\]

The percentage change in wavelength is:
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_{\text{scatt}}} \times 100\% = \frac{4.17 \times 10^{-7}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100\% = 20.85\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{20.85\%}\).
