","The coefficient of induced magnetization \( \kappa_1 \) of a medium is affected by its surroundings. When a medium with \( \kappa_1 \) is surrounded by another medium with \( \kappa' \), the resulting value of \( \kappa_1 \) depends on the relative permittivity (\(\varepsilon_1\) and \( \epsilon'_r \)) and relative permeability (\(\mu_1\) and \( \mu'_r \)) of the two media. The effect arises because the magnetic dipole moment of the first medium is influenced by the external magnetic field created by the second medium.

