The net consists of 1 mm-diameter strings forming 1 cm squares. The drag coefficient depends on the effective surface area of the net, which is determined by the number of strands and their spacing.

- The net is made of 1 mm-diameter strings.
- The squares are 1 cm (10 mm) on a side.
- The strands are spaced 1 cm apart, so the effective surface area is not the total area of the net.

The effective surface area (Ae) is given by:
\[
Ae = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{\text{Number of strands per square cm}}{\text{Number of squares per cm}^2} \times \text{Area of the net}
\]

The number of strands per square cm is 10 (since the strings are 1 mm diameter and spaced 1 cm apart).
The number of squares per cm^2 is 100 (since the squares are 1 cm on a side).

Thus, the effective surface area is:
\[
\frac{10}{100} = 0.1
\]

This means the effective surface area is 10% of the actual surface area.

