","The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is given by the uncertainty in the measurement. The uncertainty arises from the short lifetime of the particle, which limits the precision of the measurement. The uncertainty is given by the uncertainty principle, which states that the uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) is inversely proportional to the lifetime (\(\tau\)):

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{h}{2\tau}
\]

where \(h\) is Planck's constant (\(6.635 \times 10^{-34} \text{ eV s}\)).

Substituting the given lifetime \(\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\):

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{6.635 \times 10^{-34} \text{ eV s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 1.65875 \times 10^{-11} \text{ eV}
\]

To convert this to MeV, divide by \(1.91 \times 10^3 \text{ eV/MeV}\):

\[
\Delta E \geq 1.65875 \times 10^{-11} \text{ eV} \times \frac{1}{1.91 \times 10^3 \text{ eV/MeV}} \approx 8.4 \times 10^{-15} \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the uncertainty in the rest energy is approximately \(8.4 \times 10^{-15} \text{ MeV}\).

The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:

\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 8.4 \times 10^{-15} \text{ MeV}
\]

Since the uncertainty is very small compared to the rest energy, the measurement is very precise.

