","To calculate the overall entropy increase, we use the principle of entropy change:  
\[
\Delta S = \Delta S_{gas} + \Delta S_{water}
\]
Given:  
- Gas cools from \( T_1 = 1100^\text{C} = 1373 \text{ K} \) to \( T_2 = 550^\text{C} = 823 \text{ K} \).  
- Water vaporizes at \( T = 220^\text{C} = 493 \text{ K} \).  
- Specific heat of gas \( c_p = 1.005 \text{ kJ/kg K} \).  
- Latent heat of water \( L = 1858.5 \text{ kJ/kg} \).  
- Mass of water \( m_{water} = 1 \text{ kg} \).  

1. **Entropy change of the gas (\(\Delta S_{gas}\)):**  
   The gas cools, and the heat lost is:  
   \[
   \Delta H_{gas} = m_{gas} c_p (T_1 - T_2)
   \]  
   Since all heat transferred from the gas goes to the water, the heat lost by the gas equals the heat gained by the water.  
   The heat gained by the water is:  
   \[
   \Delta H_{water} = m_{water} L = 1 \text{ kg} \times 1858.5 \text{ kJ/kg} = 1858.5 \text{ kJ}
   \]  
   Thus, \( \Delta H_{gas} = 1858.5 \text{ kJ} \).  
   The mass of the gas is:  
   \[
   m_{gas} = \frac{\Delta H_{gas}}{c_p (T_1 - T_2)} = \frac{1858.5 \text{ kJ}}{1.005 \text{ kJ/kg K} \times (1373 - 873)} = \frac{1858.5}{1005 \times 500} = 0.377 \text{ kg}
   \]  
   The entropy change of the gas is:  
   \[
   \Delta S_{gas} = m_{gas} c_p \ln\left(\frac{T_1}{T_2}\right) = 0.377 \text{ kg} \times 1.005 \text{ kJ/kg K} \times \ln\left(\frac{1373}{873}\right) = 0.377 \times 1.005 \times 0.525 = 0.200 \text{ kJ/K}
   \]  

2. **Entropy change of the water (\(\Delta S_{water}\)):**  
   The water vaporizes at constant temperature, so the entropy change is:  
   \[
   \Delta S_{water} = m_{water} \left(\frac{L}{T_v}\right) = 1 \text{ kg} \times \left(\frac{1858.5 \text{ kJ}}{493 \text{ K}}\right) = 3.81 \text{ kJ/K}
